{
  "text": " I am Dr. James Buckmaster. I'm from a little town in western Kentucky. I'm a sole practitioner. I've been there for about 40 years. Between, well, I just had my wife and myself to run the practice. Some of our staff left early because they had to go home and take care of their children. And I, you know, once they closed the schools. So it was just her and myself and we've treated, well, we've worked 14 hour days probably for the last year. And in the last two months, we've worked 14 hours a day, seven days a week. And we've come to somewhere around the number of 7000 patients that we have treated successfully for COVID. And our patients, most of them, of course, Henderson is not that big. We didn't have 7000 people there, but we had a lot of telehealth people that came to call this. And, you know, I've worked harder now than I did as an intern. And I'm trying to retire. So it's just, my whole career has flipped upside down. And I was shaking my head thinking, I just want to go home, you know. But anyway, you know, I was, I was created to heal. And that's what I was going to do. And if it meant my own life, I was going to do it. And now, you know, after 40 years of practice, my license is on the line. And it's the second time around for me. But I will die on this hill. I've heard the gentlemen speak earlier. I've walked the same walk. I feel like I've been represented. I've had patients that were just amazing. I have one guy that he basically was in intensive care unit at a small hospital. And he ripped out his intubation tube and walked out of the hospital. And he called me. And I was like, okay. So where are we going to go from here? I thought you had some ideas. So anyway, we started treating I threw everything at me as a big guy. That's one thing I noticed that, you know, bigger people got sick or so I was very aggressive about treating. I put him on ivermectin hydroxychloroquine. We put him on high doses of zinc. You know, everything I could think of. And he's he's home today. He's off to oxygen. And he's I told him go back and visit the people that asked you and say, hey, I made it. So that, you know, it just it's one of those things that I try to encourage my patients who have been successful and and my patriot pharmacists have gone out on the limb to do the treatment to go back to them, go back to that pharmacist and tell them thank you. Thank you for saving your life because if they weren't there, we weren't we're gonna be able to do it. And you do that if you've been saved by your pharmacist, go back when everybody's looking for a way to fight this battle. And I'm gonna give you a weapon before I leave. Okay. The only way you can beat these people is by hitting them right in the wallet. Okay. So get your prescriptions and go to your mom and pop pharmacies because they're the ones that are helping you. Okay. The other guys. The other guys are gonna let you down their parking lot. And I tell patients over and over and they're like, gosh, it's so much more expensive there. You know, I have to, you know, take all my prescriptions over. I said, well, freedom has a cost. And that's it, you're gonna pay a little higher maybe but that you're still gonna be free because if we didn't have those little mom and pop pharmacies, I couldn't do a thing. I could not do a thing. So you think those young men and women and older guys out there that still have their little shop out there because they're saving lives every day. And if it wasn't for them, I couldn't treat people in Tennessee or people wherever else they call from because those places without them, I tell them we'd all be dead. So thank you for listening. I appreciate you know, you guys that asked me to come down.",
